# CleanMatch Web App — Project Rules

## Stack
- Next.js 14 App Router (TypeScript, strict mode)
- Tailwind CSS for all styling
- Supabase (Auth via SSR, PostgreSQL, Realtime, Storage)
- Stripe Connect (destination charges, 15% platform commission)
- shadcn/ui for component library
- Deployed on Vercel

## Routing Structure
app/
  (auth)/login/page.tsx
  (auth)/register/page.tsx
  (client)/home/page.tsx
  (client)/book/page.tsx
  (client)/bookings/page.tsx
  (cleaner)/dashboard/page.tsx
  (cleaner)/jobs/[id]/page.tsx
  api/stripe/webhook/route.ts
  api/stripe/create-payment-intent/route.ts
  api/intake/route.ts

## Intake estimator
Use a simple deterministic estimator that calculates estimated_hours and suggested price range from bedrooms, bathrooms, and clean type. No external AI APIs.

## Business Logic
- Two roles: CLIENT and CLEANER
- Cleaners set hourly rate; platform adds 15% service fee shown upfront before booking
- Booking statuses: pending → confirmed → in_progress → completed → disputed → cancelled
- Match score = proximity (40%) + rating (30%) + recency (20%) + preference_match (10%)
- No automated deactivation. All flags require human review.

## Code Rules
- Always use Supabase SSR client (@supabase/ssr) for server components and route handlers
- Use Supabase browser client only in client components
- Enable RLS on every Supabase table — no exceptions
- Never expose SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY or STRIPE_SECRET_KEY outside of /app/api/ or Edge Functions
- Use server actions for form mutations where possible
- shadcn/ui components only — do not install other UI libraries
- Handle errors at top of functions (early return pattern)
- TypeScript types must be generated from Supabase schema — no manual type writing
- Commit after every working feature
